 "response": "Driril's budget is **$1528K**. I counted only the Finance Office's budget changes and applied its withdrawals as the policy describes.\n\n**Method**\n1. Starting point: the Finance Office set the budget to $1630K in #0001.\n2. I listed every Finance Office withdrawal and resolved the chains from the latest back to the earliest.\n   - A withdrawal that is itself withdrawn has no effect.\n   - That restores the update it had targeted.\n   - Examples:\n     - #1596 cancels #1006 \u2192 #0254 stands \u2192 #0225 is cancelled \u2192 the \u2212$571K in #0214 applies.\n     - #0566 cancels #0116 \u2192 #0047 stands \u2192 #0032 is cancelled \u2192 the +$502K in #0024 applies.\n     - #1039 cancels #0924 \u2192 #0720 stands \u2192 #0631 is cancelled \u2192 #0571 stands \u2192 the \u2212$32K in #0473 does not apply.\n3. I then applied every remaining Finance Office increase and decrease, in record order, to the $1630K starting figure.\n4. Everything else was ignored: messages from Sales, Vendor, PMO, Legal, Operations, Marketing, Engineering and Facilities. Finance Office notes that don't change the budget (room moves, audit notes, closed purchase orders) were also left out.\n\nThe final running total after #1600 (+$85K) is $1528K.\n\nThis was a long hand calculation over about 1,600 records. The method is sound, but a single slip in the running total would change the result.\n\nANSWER: $1528K",
